.TH "PERFORMANCERESOURCETIMING.DECODEDBODYSIZE" "3JS" "October 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceResourceTiming.decodedBodySize \- PerformanceResourceTiming:
decodedBodySize property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]decodedBodySize\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property returns the
size (in octets) received from the fetch (HTTP or cache) of the message
body after removing any applied content encoding (like gzip or Brotli).
.SH VALUE
The \f[CR]decodedBodySize\f[R] property can have the following values:
.IP \(bu 2
A number representing the size (in octets) received from the fetch (HTTP
or cache) of the message body, after removing any applied content
encoding.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]0\f[R] if the resource is a cross\-origin request made in
\f[CR]no\-cors\f[R] mode, or if the request failed, for example, because
it didn\(cqt pass the CORS check.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Content size information for a cross\-origin resource is restricted
unless the resource passes the CORS check, so if the value of the
\f[CR]decodedBodySize\f[R] property is \f[CR]0\f[R], the resource might
be a cross\-origin request.
.PP
To expose cross\-origin content size information, the resource must be
requested in \f[CR]cors\f[R] mode, and the response must pass the CORS
check, which requires it to include an appropriate
\f[CR]Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin\f[R] header.
Requests made with \f[CR]fetch()\f[R] use \f[CR]cors\f[R] mode by
default.
Elements such as \f[CR]<img>\f[R] use \f[CR]no\-cors\f[R] mode unless
the \f[CR]crossorigin\f[R] attribute is set, so a cross\-origin image
loaded without it has a content size of \f[CR]0\f[R].
.PP
To allow \f[CR]https://developer.mozilla.org\f[R] to see content sizes,
the cross\-origin resource should send:
.IP
.EX
Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin: https://developer.mozilla.org
.EE
.PP
Browsers are allowed to apply stricter restrictions than CORS requires,
and may return \f[CR]0\f[R] even when the CORS check passes.
.PP
Frame navigations, such as an \f[CR]<iframe>\f[R] loading its document,
don\(cqt use CORS.
Their content sizes are exposed only for same\-origin frames that were
loaded without cross\-origin redirects, even if the frame\(cqs document
passes the \f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R] check.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Checking if content was compressed
If the \f[CR]decodedBodySize\f[R] and \f[CR]encodedBodySize\f[R]
properties are non\-null and differ, the content was compressed (for
example, gzip or Brotli).
.PP
Example using a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R], which notifies of new
\f[CR]resource\f[R] performance entries as they are recorded in the
browser\(cqs performance timeline.
Use the \f[CR]buffered\f[R] option to access entries from before the
observer creation.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  list.getEntries().forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] uncompressed =
      entry.decodedBodySize && entry.decodedBodySize === entry.encodedBodySize;
    \f[B]if\f[R] (uncompressed) {
      console.log(\(ga${entry.name} was not compressed!\(ga);
    }
  });
});

observer.observe({ type: \(dqresource\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
.EE
.PP
Example using \f[CR]Performance.getEntriesByType()\f[R], which only
shows \f[CR]resource\f[R] performance entries present in the
browser\(cqs performance timeline at the time you call this method:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] resources = performance.getEntriesByType(\(dqresource\(dq);
resources.forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] uncompressed =
    entry.decodedBodySize && entry.decodedBodySize === entry.encodedBodySize;
  \f[B]if\f[R] (uncompressed) {
    console.log(\(ga${entry.name} was not compressed!\(ga);
  }
});
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Cross\-origin resource sharing (CORS)
